{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788350814,"nonce":"xtMBS2HthH2aucsVhweN3zWX2M4=","title":"**Promoted from a comment on `d2e1229`.** Run `m1-20260901`: `Alert_ChessTableNoChairs` sat **live for 13 in-game days**, printed in every day snapshot, neither fixed nor muted. Nothing requires a verdict on an active alert, so an alert can be read every single turn and acted on never.","message":"The mod has the two ends of this and not the middle. `alert-mute` is a deliberate, reasoned act with a required `reason`, and `digest` is explicitly described in `AlertMuteVerbs.cs` as *\"what keeps it from being forgotten\"*. But an active alert row carries **no age**, so \"this has been live for 13 days\" is not a fact any reader can state. `AlertScanner` emits `alert_on` at a tick and retains nothing.\n\n\nAn alert's age is computable and currently discarded. `AlertScanner` already notices the transition; it needs to keep the tick.\n\n\n1. Each `alerts.active` row publishes `first_seen_tick` and an age in ticks and in days. An alert that clears and returns starts a new age, and the fact that it is a return is visible rather than being presented as a fresh alert.\n2. Age survives the scan cadence honestly: `AlertScanner.cs:12` already documents that a scan tick trails the causing tick by design. The age field carries the same caveat rather than implying a precision it does not have.\n3. `active[*].muted` continues to read as it does today; age and mute are orthogonal and an old muted alert is a normal, fine state.\n4. `checklists/turn.md` gains the trip-wire: an `alerts.active` id that is neither muted nor named in a ledger `action` within **N day-boundary reads** is flagged for a verdict. This is `OUT-3` of `RUNS/m1-20260901/postmortem.md`, which was proposed and never landed.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-alert-age.py` on a bench: raise an alert, advance across day boundaries, assert the age grows and matches the tick arithmetic; mute it, assert the age is unaffected; clear and re-raise it, assert the age resets and the return is visible.\n\n\nThe verdict rule is general. `Alert_ChessTableNoChairs` was harmless. The same silence covered every other alert the run declined to answer, and the postmortem's four-instance letter/alert defect is the same shape: a signal that is read and never adjudicated.","files":null}]}